; The treasure bonus writer, with the First Finisher figure raised to 100 when
; the switch file is there.
;
; Half of a feature a build can leave out -- the other half is the switch,
; first_finisher_100.jaf beside it, and feature.js in this folder is where the
; pair is named. That file is also where the +100 and its arithmetic live; this
; one is about where the hook goes.
;
; BattleBonusCollection@Set#1 (FUNC 27277) is the three-argument
; Set(type, value, id) that stores one treasure/experience bonus into the
; collection -- BattleBonusCalculator::CalcTreasure calls it directly for the
; First Finisher (Set(初トドメ, 50, id)) and the two-argument Set (FUNC 27276)
; funnels every other bonus through it with id "". So it sees every bonus, which
; is why the substitution is gated on the type: 初トドメ is BattleBonusType 8 --
; the PUSH 8 in CalcTreasure's call and the only value the game ever gives that
; slot -- and every other type falls straight through with its own figure. See
; docs/treasure-chest-chance.md for the five bonuses and the roll.
;
; WHAT THE PREFIX DOES. Before the original body runs, and only when type is 8,
; the local `value` (argument 1) is replaced with
;   FirstFinisher100Value(value)
; which is 100 with the switch file present and `value` -- the shipped 50 --
; without it. The rest of the function then stores and adjusts that number as it
; always did, so the result screen's First Finisher line and the total both read
; the same figure and still add up. This is the "narrower knob"
; docs/treasure-chest-chance.md names: a different figure than 50, without
; touching the other four bonuses or the displayed sum.
;
; WHY A .JAM. The figure is the `value` argument to a method whose first
; parameter is the enum BattleBonusType, which alice-tools' .jaf compiler will
; not name -- so an `override` of Set#1 cannot be written as source at all (a
; `BattleBonusType type` parameter is a parse error; an `int type` one fails the
; type check the moment super() is handed it back). A .jam assembles a whole
; function and repoints the table at it, past the type system entirely. It adds
; no locals -- --jam rewrites code and not the FUNC section -- and needs none:
; the prefix reuses the function's own `type` and `value` arguments.
;
; The body below is BattleBonusCollection@Set#1 verbatim from
;   alice ain dump -c game/ain/Rance10.v1.04.ain
; with the four-instruction prefix in front of it. Regenerate it the same way if
; the game version changes: 27277, 27284 (Find) and 27283 (AdjustBonusValues)
; are indices into one build of one .ain, PUSH 8 is 初トドメ's place in one build
; of the enum, and both would mean something else in the next.
;
; Save this file as UTF-8 with no BOM. The assembler reads a byte order mark as
; an instruction and stops on line 1, and an editor that defaults .jam to
; UTF-8-with-BOM will quietly put one back.
;
;   if (type == 8) value = FirstFinisher100Value(value);   <- the prefix
FUNC 27277
	.LOCALREF type
	PUSH 8
	NOTE
	IFNZ ff100_skip
	PUSHLOCALPAGE
	PUSH 1
	.LOCALREF value
	CALLFUNC FirstFinisher100Value
	ASSIGN
	POP
ff100_skip:
	PUSHLOCALPAGE
	PUSH 3
	PUSHSTRUCTPAGE
	PUSH 27284
	.LOCALREF type
	CALLMETHOD 1
	ASSIGN
	POP
	.LOCALREF index
	PUSH -1
	NOTE
	IFNZ 0x42ebb8
	JUMP 0x42ec16
0x42ebb8:
	.STRUCTREF BattleBonusCollection m_bonus
	.LOCALREF index
	REF
	PUSH 1
	.LOCALREF value
	ASSIGN
	POP
	.STRUCTREF BattleBonusCollection m_bonus
	.LOCALREF index
	REF
	PUSH 2
	REF
	.LOCALREF id
	A_REF
	S_ASSIGN
	DELETE
	JUMP 0x42ecd2
0x42ec16:
	.LOCALCREATE b BattleBonus
	.LOCALREF b
	PUSH 0
	.LOCALREF type
	ASSIGN
	POP
	.LOCALREF b
	PUSH 1
	.LOCALREF value
	ASSIGN
	POP
	.LOCALREF b
	PUSH 2
	REF
	.LOCALREF id
	A_REF
	S_ASSIGN
	DELETE
	.STRUCTREF BattleBonusCollection m_bonus
	.LOCALREF b
	A_REF
	CALLHLL Array PushBack 13
	.LOCALDELETE b
0x42ecd2:
	PUSHSTRUCTPAGE
	PUSH 27283
	CALLMETHOD 0
	RETURN
ENDFUNC BattleBonusCollection@Set#1
